<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Repo-wide hang guard for `dotnet test`.

  Problem (2026-09-27): CI (.github/workflows/ci.yml) and tools/verify-all.ps1 always pass the CLI
  flags blame-hang, blame-hang-timeout 120s and blame-hang-dump-type none, but a raw
  `dotnet test PhotoReview.slnx ...` (the command agents and AGENTS.md "Quick Checks" actually run
  day to day) has no hang guard at all. A single deadlocked UI test then hangs the whole session
  indefinitely (observed: 80+ minutes) instead of failing fast.

  This file is wired into every project under tests/ via tests/Directory.Build.props
  (RunSettingsFilePath), so it applies automatically to `dotnet test` even with no extra flags.

  TestSessionTimeout: 1200000 ms = 20 minutes per `dotnet test` *session* (one process, i.e. one
  project). Measured wall-clock on this machine (Release, no-build, default filter
  Category!=Manual&Category!=Native&Category!=Slow):
    - PhotoReview.Architecture.Tests / Core.Tests / Imaging.Tests / App.Tests: each well under 60s.
    - PhotoReview.Integration.Tests (the slowest normal project, has the real WPF/UI tests): ~70s.
    - Whole filtered suite across all 5 projects sequentially: ~3 minutes.
  20 minutes is well over 10x the slowest single-project run observed, so it will not fire on a
  healthy run, but it still bounds a `dotnet test` invocation that doesn't hang on any one test yet
  somehow never finishes (e.g. a livelock across many tests). It is intentionally not tight to the
  ~70s figure: that number moves with machine load (this repo is routinely built with several
  concurrent agent sessions), and TestSessionTimeout aborts the whole session (not just the
  offending test), so it must stay generous. The per-test hang is what actually matters day to day,
  and that is bounded to 120s by the blame collector below, independent of this session cap.

  Native/Slow full runs (tools/verify-all.ps1 -Native / -Slow) are not bounded tighter by this file:
  those suites include intentionally slow real-OS tests and can legitimately run longer in
  aggregate, but verify-all.ps1 still invokes `dotnet test` once per project even in -Native/-Slow
  mode, and each project's slowest observed run (including Slow-tagged tests) stayed well under the
  20-minute cap locally, so no single session should approach it. If a future Slow/Native run ever
  needs more headroom, override per-invocation on the command line with
  RunConfiguration.TestSessionTimeout=<ms> after a literal double dash, rather than raising this
  file's default for every other run.

  Blame hang collector: kills and *names* an individual hung test after 120s, matching the CLI
  blame-hang-timeout 120s / blame-hang-dump-type none flags already used by CI and verify-all.ps1.
  Having it here means a bare `dotnet test` (no CLI flags at all) gets the same guard. CI and
  verify-all.ps1 keep passing their own blame-hang* flags too; VSTest treats the explicit CLI blame
  flags as the effective configuration when both are present (they configure the same "blame" data
  collector under the hood) rather than erroring on a duplicate collector. Verified empirically for
  this repo; see docs/refactoring/decisions/TEST-HANG-GUARD.md for the exact command and output.
-->
<RunSettings>
  <RunConfiguration>
    <TestSessionTimeout>1200000</TestSessionTimeout>
  </RunConfiguration>
  <DataCollectionRunSettings>
    <DataCollectors>
      <DataCollector friendlyName="blame" enabled="True">
        <Configuration>
          <CollectDumpOnTestSessionHang TestTimeout="120s" HangDumpType="None" />
        </Configuration>
      </DataCollector>
    </DataCollectors>
  </DataCollectionRunSettings>
</RunSettings>
